          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"translation_en\":\"ROXANE:\\n  Oh! **I’m very fond of you**. I must go.\\n  (She quickly puts her mask back on, with a lace veil over her brow, and, absentmindedly):\\n  But you haven’t told me about last night’s battle.\\n  Truly, it must have been extraordinary!...\\n  Tell him to write to me.\\n  (She blows him a little kiss with her hand):\\n  Oh! I love you!\",\"larger_context_summary\":\"Roxane is speaking to her cousin Cyrano after confiding that she loves the handsome young cadet Christian. She asks Cyrano to protect Christian and serve as an intermediary, unaware that Cyrano himself is secretly in love with her. Her request that “he” write refers to Christian and reflects her expectation of romantic eloquence—an expectation Cyrano will help Christian satisfy by composing letters for him. Roxane is preparing to leave and warmly thanks Cyrano; meanwhile, her casual mention of his remarkable fight the previous night underscores how little she understands either his devotion or his emotional pain.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The target is the qualified French phrase “je vous aime bien,” which ordinarily avows liking or warm affection rather than making the stronger romantic declaration suggested by unqualified “je vous aime.” It therefore naturally functions as a sincere claim about Roxane’s feelings, especially given her trusting, affectionate relationship with Cyrano. It does not itself promise future conduct, redefine their relationship, or undertake an obligation; any interpersonal consequences arise from the painful dramatic context rather than from a performative act. The exclamation “Oh!,” Roxane’s hurried movements, and her later blown kiss heighten the warmth and intensity of the scene, but intensity is not the same as reflexive or involuntary expressive force. The later “Oh! je vous aime!” is a separate, stronger-sounding repetition and is not the annotated occurrence. Her simultaneous request for Christian’s letter further clarifies that her affection for Cyrano is distinct from her romantic attachment.\"}"
        }
